{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1591 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "64f72c62d5f134b11d489f6084f24f96c86cb2411abfa8849430c4b8846b1fe9" :edb/db-before-t 1590 :edb/db-after-t 1591 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534903 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:33:23.214Z" 13194139534903 true] [17592186128914 1100 "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2)$." 13194139534903 false] [17592186128914 1100 "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186128914 1145 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\n\\ln (x^{2} + 2y^{2} - z^{2}) &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - z^{2} &= e^{c} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{e^{c}} + \\frac{2y^{2}}{e^{c}} - \\frac{z^{2}}{e^{c}} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} = 1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$" 13194139534903 false] [17592186128914 1145 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186128915 1100 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$." 13194139534903 false] [17592186128915 1100 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186128915 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\n\\sin xyz &= 1 \\\\\nxyz &= \\frac{π}{2} + 2πk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis the surface\n\n$$\nxyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer." 13194139534903 false] [17592186128915 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz & =1 \\\\ xyz & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the surface $xyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k,$ where $k$ is any integer." 13194139534903 true] [17592186128916 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z$, find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0$." 13194139534903 false] [17592186128916 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186128916 1145 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0$. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - z &= 0 \\\\\nz &= x^{2} + 4y^{2} \\\\\nz &= x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{1/4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nz = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}\n$$" 13194139534903 false] [17592186128916 1145 "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186129096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318575 13194139534903 true] [17592186129096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318582 13194139534903 true] [17592186129096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318589 13194139534903 true] [17592186129096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318610 13194139534903 true] [17592186129096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318617 13194139534903 true] [17592186129097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318561 13194139534903 true] [17592186129097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318568 13194139534903 true] [17592186129097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318624 13194139534903 true] [17592186129097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318631 13194139534903 true] [17592186129098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318540 13194139534903 true] [17592186129098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318547 13194139534903 true] [17592186129098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318554 13194139534903 true] [17592186129098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318596 13194139534903 true] [17592186129098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318603 13194139534903 true] [17592186129543 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534903 true] [17592186129543 1100 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x, y, z) = \\ln (x + 2y + 3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1, 1, 0)$." 13194139534903 false] [17592186129543 1100 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,0).$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186129543 1145 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2(1)+3(0))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x+2y+3z & =3 \\\\ x+2y+3z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ is the plane $x+2y+3z-3=0.$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318541 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1089 "q-129182" 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1113 #uuid "aaa065e5-4d42-53ad-a1bb-ce64f0d24542" 13194139534903 true] [17592186318540 1145 "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$" 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318542 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318543 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318544 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318545 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318546 13194139534903 true] [17592186318541 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 1319413953